' the BIOS title screen is skipped: the game opens on its own menu
'cartridge "SKIING/CLAUDE FOR AMY STUDIO/2026"

' SKIING
' The ski game the ColecoVision catalog promised: "Ski Trails" down open
' mountainsides, round trees, moguls and other skiers who cut across your
' path, and "Slalom" through gates against the clock - seen from behind
' your skis, over a slope that rises and falls. Written in Amy by Claude
' (an AI made by Anthropic) for Amy Studio, 2026, on the engine of
' Avalanche Run, which comes along as the third game: its story mode.
'
' Liberties and sources: Coleco's catalog announced a two-in-one SKIING
' cartridge (Ski Trails and Slalom) whose pictures showed a 3-D slope; as
' far as we know it never came out. Only the catalog's description guided
' this game: the course, the hills, the drift, the music and every picture
' are new. Avalanche Run (inspired by AVALANCHE in Tim Hartnell's Giant
' Book of Computer Games) is unchanged but for the hills and the drift.
'
' Modes: AVALANCHE RUN (three stages ahead of an avalanche, as before),
' SKI TRAILS (one long run: trees, rocks and crossing skiers; the clock),
' SLALOM (gates; a missed gate costs five seconds).
'
' Controls (either joystick): left and right steer - the faster you go, the
' less the skis bite, so you drift wide and throw up snow; up tucks; down
' snowploughs. Every hill has a big hump and a small one. The big crest
' taken fast throws you into the air (the small one gives a short hop):
' no steering
' until you land, and you fly over what is below. Hold a button in the
' air to cross the skis (a trick: 50 points, or a second off the clock if
' held a while, with a fanfare and a sparkle flying to the scoreboard),
' but let go before landing or you fall. In the air a soft hum (straight
' to the sound chip's third tone channel, free during a run) rises and
' falls with you. Either button chooses on the menu: PLAY or CREDITS, then
' the game, then START, HOW TO PLAY (that game's rules) or BACK. No BIOS
' title screen (the cartridge line is commented out). Keypad not used.
'
' How the hills work: art.py casts a ray through every pixel line of the
' ground for 32 places along a hill (two humps in every 128 metres), from a
' camera pitched to the slope; each place has its own line depths (255:
' hidden past a crest), row scales and object lines. HillPhase points the
' Z80 routines at the right ones as Pos runs.
'
' House rules kept: text in columns 2-29 and rows 1-22 (the ground and
' mountains run to the edges, as pictures); eleven sprites: the two ski
' tips, four objects, the spray and, in the air, the skis' back
' halves and the two boots (sprites are magnified: 32 x 32 on the screen,
' so the back halves go 32 lines lower; the airborne skis are 3D models projected to pixels by
' ski3b.py: a pixel is set when enough of it is covered); the frame loop
' is protected from
' the NMI, and every screen is drawn with the NMI off after a wait.
'
' How the picture works: rows 8-23 are the ground. Each row uses its own
' four tiles (snow, forest, left edge, right edge) in its third of the
' screen. The four share one colour per pixel line, forest in front and
' snow behind, so one 32-byte copy recolours a row. Every frame each pixel
' line takes its depth from a table, adds the distance run, and turns grey
' or white: the snow bands rush toward you. Every frame each row also gets
' its forest edges from the player's place, the piste's width and the
' curve (which reaches further at a distance); they move in half tiles.

